# Grade 5: teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'5-1-1': dict(
  saint="Leo the Great: the pope who said that what was visible in Christ has passed into the sacraments.",
  tryit=["Match each sacrament to its matter and form.", "Say who acts in each.", "Explain how the sign works."],
  saintStory="Leo was born in Italy around the year 400 and became pope in 440. At that time Rome was in great danger from barbarian invaders. In 452, when Attila the Hun marched toward Rome, Leo went out to meet him, and Attila turned back. In 455 he also persuaded the Vandals not to burn the city.\nLeo was also a great teacher. He wrote a letter, called the Tome, explaining that Jesus is one Person with two natures, divine and human. When it was read at the Council of Chalcedon in 451, the bishops said, “Peter has spoken through Leo.”\nIn one of his sermons he wrote that what was visible in our Redeemer has passed into the sacraments. That is the idea of this lesson: the Jesus who touched and healed people now acts in the sacraments.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read Luke 5:17-26 aloud, or tell it, while two children act: friends lower a man through a roof to Jesus. Stop at the moment Jesus speaks. Ask, “Why did he forgive the man’s sins first, and heal his legs second? What was he showing?”",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus did not only teach. He touched. He put mud on a blind man’s eyes. He took a girl by the hand. A woman touched his cloak and was healed. Jesus’ body was the way God’s love reached people. After Jesus rose and ascended, he did not stop touching us. He does it through the Church’s sacraments. Pope St. Leo said that what was visible in Christ passed into the sacraments.”", "Say: “A sacrament is an outward sign, instituted by Christ, that gives the grace it signifies. There are always two parts, the sign and the words. The sign is called the matter, such as water, bread, wine or oil. The words are called the form, such as ‘I baptize you…’ and ‘This is my body.’ When a validly ordained minister celebrates the sacrament with the proper matter and form, and the right intention to do what the Church does, Christ himself acts.”", "Say: “That is why the sacraments work no matter how holy or unholy the minister is: the true minister is Christ. But the sacraments are not magic. We receive their fruits according to our faith and readiness. The Church herself is called the sacrament of Christ’s presence, because she is the visible sign through which he acts in the world.”"],
    ask=["What are the two parts of a sacrament? (The sign, or matter; the words, or form.)", "Who is the real minister of every sacrament? (Christ.)", "Why are the sacraments not magic? (We must receive them with faith and openness.)"],
    practice=["Set out cards: Baptism (water; “I baptize you…”), Eucharist (bread and wine; “This is my body…”), Confirmation (chrism; “Be sealed…”), Anointing (oil; “Through this holy anointing…”), Reconciliation (words of absolution), Orders (laying on of hands), Matrimony (consent).", "In teams, match the sacrament to its matter and its form.", "For each, add: how is Jesus acting? (He washes, feeds, strengthens, heals, forgives, sends, binds.)", "Share and check against a Catechism summary."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, you still touch us through the sacraments. Thank you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Name one sacrament and what Jesus does in it.”")),

'5-1-2': dict(
  saint="Charles Borromeo: an archbishop who built seminaries and fed the plague-stricken.",
  tryit=["Draw a life path from birth to old age.", "Mark where each sacrament helps.", "Explain the three groups."],
  saintStory="Charles Borromeo was born in 1538 into a noble family in northern Italy. When he was twenty-two, his uncle, who was pope, made him a cardinal, and Charles was not sure what that was for. After his older brother died, he decided to live as a priest should, and he took his responsibilities seriously.\nIn 1564 he became archbishop of Milan. He visited all the parishes in his large diocese, even in the high Alps. He started seminaries to train priests well, schools of Christian doctrine for children on Sundays, and carefully taught that the sacraments should be celebrated with reverence. In 1576, when the plague came to Milan, he stayed. He organized food, care for the sick and prayer, and walked barefoot in procession.\nCharles died in 1584, at forty-six. He shows how the sacraments, in every stage of life, reach the whole people of God.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a long line on the board marked with ages 0, 10, 20, 40, 70. Ask, “What big things happen in a human life?” Write the answers on the line: birth, growing up, getting hurt, getting sick, falling in love, working, getting old. Say, “Notice how many of these God has a sacrament for.”",
    teach=["Say: “There are seven sacraments, and they fit the three great needs of life. First, we need life and growth. The sacraments of initiation give it: Baptism gives new life, Confirmation strengthens us, and the Eucharist feeds us. The Catechism says they lay the foundations of every Christian life. Peter said on Pentecost, ‘Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of your sins.’ (Acts 2:38)”", "Say: “Second, we need healing. We get sick, and we sin. The sacraments of healing are Reconciliation, which forgives our sins and reconciles us to God and the Church, and the Anointing of the Sick, which strengthens us in serious illness or old age. Third, we need to serve one another and build up the Church. The sacraments at the service of communion are Holy Orders, in which men are ordained as bishops, priests and deacons to serve God’s people, and Matrimony, in which a man and a woman give themselves to each other and to their children.”", "Say: “Not everyone receives all seven. Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist are for everyone. Holy Orders and Matrimony are for a particular calling. Orders and Baptism and Confirmation can only be received once, because they mark the soul forever. The Eucharist and Reconciliation we can receive again and again. Each sacrament meets us just where we are.”"],
    ask=["What are the three groups of sacraments? (Initiation, healing, service of communion.)", "Which sacraments can we receive only once? (Baptism, Confirmation, Holy Orders.)", "Why do we need sacraments of healing? (We get sick and we sin.)"],
    practice=["Give each student a long sheet. Draw a winding life path from birth to old age.", "Mark where each sacrament fits: birth (Baptism), growing up (Confirmation, Eucharist), when we fall (Reconciliation), when we are sick (Anointing), vocation (Orders or Matrimony).", "Color-code the three groups.", "Explain your life map to a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, thank you for meeting us at every stage of life.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Explain the three groups of sacraments to someone.”")),

'5-1-3': dict(
  saint="Helena: the empress who sought the cross of Jesus and built churches.",
  tryit=["Visit the symbol museum.", "Explain one object to a partner.", "Say what it shows about God."],
  saintStory="Helena was born around the year 250, probably to a poor family in the Roman Empire. She married a Roman officer, and their son was Constantine, who became emperor. Constantine made Christianity legal and allowed Christians to worship in peace.\nWhen Helena was about seventy-five, she traveled to the Holy Land as a pilgrim. She visited the places where Jesus lived, and she helped to build churches over the places of his birth in Bethlehem and his ascension on the Mount of Olives. According to tradition she also discovered the cross of Jesus in Jerusalem.\nHelena shows how we use signs and holy places to remember Jesus. The cross is the greatest Christian symbol, and Helena’s love for it reminds us how much the signs of our faith matter.",
  script=dict(
    open="Set out a mystery bag with objects: a candle, a small dish of water, a cross, a piece of bread, a bottle of oil, a stole, some incense. Pull them out one at a time. Ask, “What do you use this for? Where have you seen it?”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church’s liturgy uses signs and symbols. A symbol is something visible that points to something invisible. God made us body and soul, so we learn about invisible things through visible ones. Many of the symbols come from creation: water, oil, fire, bread, wine, light. Others come from the Bible: the cross, the lamb, the dove. And Jesus has given them new meaning.”", "Say: “Water: washes and gives life; at Baptism it shows that we are cleansed and reborn. Psalm 51:7: ‘Wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow.’ Oil: heals, strengthens and anoints; it is used for Baptism, Confirmation, Holy Orders and the Anointing of the Sick. Light: Christ is the light of the world; we light candles. Bread and wine: the food of life, they become the Body and Blood of Christ. Incense: our prayer rising to God. Psalm 141:2: ‘Let my prayer be counted as incense before you.’”", "Say: “There are also signs we do with our bodies: kneeling shows adoration, standing shows respect and readiness, bowing shows reverence, the Sign of the Cross claims us for Christ, and the laying on of hands passes on a blessing or a mission. The signs speak when words are not enough. When we understand what they mean, we pray with our whole selves.”"],
    ask=["What is a symbol? (A visible sign that points to something invisible.)", "What does water show in Baptism? (Cleansing and new life.)", "What does incense show? (Our prayer rising to God.)"],
    practice=["Set up a “symbol museum” with real objects: water, oil, a candle, a cross, bread and wine (grape juice), incense (or a picture), a white garment, a stole.", "Pairs visit each object. One explains what it is used for, what it means, and a Bible verse that goes with it. The other asks a question.", "Switch partners.", "Choose one and write a three-sentence label for the museum."],
    close="Pray Psalm 141:2: “Let my prayer be counted as incense before you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look for three signs at Mass this Sunday.”")),

'5-2-1': dict(
  saint="Peter Claver: a priest who baptized hundreds of thousands of enslaved people and treated them as children of God.",
  tryit=["Put the steps of Baptism in order.", "Say what each sign means.", "Say your own baptismal promises."],
  saintStory="Peter Claver was born in 1580 in Spain, and joined the Jesuits. He was sent to the city of Cartagena, in what is now Colombia, which was a major port for the slave trade. Thousands of men, women and children were brought there on ships from Africa, in terrible conditions.\nWhen a ship arrived, Peter went on board, even in the hold, with food, water, medicine and friends who could translate. He cared for the sick, and he taught the Christian faith and baptized them. He wanted them to know that they were beloved children of God, even though they were being treated as property. He is said to have baptized over 300,000 people in forty years. He signed his name, “Peter, slave of the slaves forever.”\nHe died in 1654. Peter Claver shows that Baptism makes every person a child of God, with dignity.",
  script=dict(
    open="Have a child pour water into a bowl while you ask, “What does water do? It can wash, it can quench thirst, it can drown, it can make things grow. In Baptism, God uses water to do something much greater.”",
    teach=["Read Romans 6:3-4. Say: “Paul wrote: ‘Do you not know that all of us who have been baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into his death? We were buried therefore with him by baptism into death, so that as Christ was raised from the dead, we too might walk in newness of life.’ Baptism joins us to Jesus’ death and Resurrection. We die to sin, and rise to a new life.”", "Say: “Baptism is the gateway to all the sacraments. It forgives original sin and all personal sins, and also all punishment for sin. It makes us children of God, temples of the Holy Spirit, and members of the Church. It leaves a permanent spiritual mark, called a character, which is why we are baptized only once. The sign is water, poured over the head three times (or the person is immersed), with the words, ‘I baptize you in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.’”", "Say: “In an emergency, anyone can baptize, using water and the Trinitarian words, with the intention of doing what the Church does. The rite includes the Sign of the Cross, readings, the prayer of exorcism (to free us from evil), the anointing with the oil of catechumens, the blessing of the water, the baptismal promises, the pouring of the water, the anointing with chrism, the white garment (a sign of new life), and a lighted candle from the Easter candle (Christ our light). Parents and godparents promise to help the child grow in faith.”"],
    ask=["What does Baptism do? (Forgives sin, makes us children of God, joins us to the Church.)", "Who can baptize in an emergency? (Anyone, with water and the right words and intention.)", "Why are we baptized only once? (It leaves a permanent mark on the soul.)"],
    practice=["Lay out cards with the steps of the Baptism rite: sign of the Cross, readings, anointing with oil of catechumens, blessing of the water, renunciation of sin and profession of faith, pouring of water, anointing with chrism, white garment, candle.", "Students put them in order and write the meaning of each sign beside it.", "Read the baptismal promises aloud, taking turns.", "Everyone answers, “I do.”"],
    close="Renew your baptismal promises together. Make the Sign of the Cross with water. Send home: “Ask when and where you were baptized, and who your godparents are.”")),

'5-2-2': dict(
  saint="Pedro Calungsod: a teenage catechist who stayed with a priest to the end.",
  tryit=["Make a card for each gift of the Spirit.", "Write what each gift helps you do.", "Choose one to ask for."],
  saintStory="Pedro Calungsod was born in the Philippines around 1654. As a teenager, he was trained by the Jesuits there as a catechist, which means a teacher of the faith. When he was about fourteen or fifteen, he joined a group of missionaries who sailed to the island of Guam, in the Pacific, to share the Gospel with the Chamorro people.\nPedro helped build churches, taught the faith, and baptized dying infants. He was strong and gentle and a skilled helper. Some people on the island did not welcome the missionaries, and in 1672 a group attacked Father Diego de San Vitores, who was baptizing a child. Pedro could have run away. Instead he stayed, and was killed with the priest.\nHe was about seventeen years old, and was named a saint in 2012. Pedro shows the courage that the Holy Spirit gives in Confirmation.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Make a bold promise. For example, ‘I will always finish what I start.’ How hard is it to keep? In Confirmation, we receive help to live out our faith boldly.”",
    teach=["Read Acts 8:14-17. Say: “When the apostles in Jerusalem heard that Samaria had accepted the word of God, they sent Peter and John. They prayed that the new believers might receive the Holy Spirit, for the Spirit had not yet come upon them. Then Peter and John laid their hands on them, and they received the Holy Spirit. This is the beginning of the sacrament of Confirmation.”", "Say: “Confirmation completes Baptism. The bishop, or a priest he delegates, anoints the forehead with chrism, a blessed oil mixed with perfume, and says, ‘Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.’ He also lays on hands. The oil means strengthening and sealing. We receive the Holy Spirit in a special way, and a permanent mark, so we can be confirmed only once. We are made stronger to live and share the faith, like soldiers of Christ.”", "Say: “The Spirit gives us seven gifts: wisdom (seeing life as God sees it), understanding (grasping the truths of faith), counsel (choosing well), fortitude (courage), knowledge (knowing God through creation and Scripture), piety (loving reverence for God), and fear of the Lord (wonder and awe before God). The fruits of the Spirit are what grow from the gifts: love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control. The confirmed are sent out to witness to Christ by their words and actions.”"],
    ask=["What does the bishop say at Confirmation? (Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.)", "Name three gifts of the Holy Spirit. (Wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety, fear of the Lord.)", "Why does Confirmation matter? (It strengthens us to live and witness to the faith.)"],
    practice=["Make seven cards, one for each gift of the Spirit.", "On each, write what the gift means in simple words and an example of using it (for instance, fortitude: standing up for someone who is bullied).", "Choose the gift you most need right now, and write a short prayer asking for it.", "Share one example with a partner."],
    close="Pray the prayer to the Holy Spirit: “Come, Holy Spirit, fill the hearts of your faithful.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask your parents about their Confirmation.”")),

'5-2-3': dict(
  saint="Frédéric Ozanam: a young layman who helped the poor and defended the faith.",
  tryit=["Write three promises: one for priest, one for prophet, one for king.", "Make them specific.", "Share one."],
  saintStory="Frédéric Ozanam was born in 1813 in Milan and grew up in Lyon, France. He was a bright student, and went to Paris to study at the Sorbonne. Many of his professors and fellow students said that the Church was out of date, and he found his faith shaken for a time. But he defended the faith with arguments and with kindness.\nWhen some students challenged him by saying that the Church did nothing for the poor, he answered, “Let us go to the poor.” In 1833, at twenty, he and a few friends started a group that visited poor families, bringing food and firewood. They took the name of Saint Vincent de Paul. It became the Society of Saint Vincent de Paul, which now has members all over the world.\nHe was also a professor, a husband and a father. He died in 1853, at forty. Frédéric shows what a lay person can do: live as priest, prophet and king through ordinary work.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Who has a job to do in the Church? Priests? The Pope? Anyone else?” Let children respond. Then say, “By Baptism, every one of us shares in Jesus’ own work. Let’s see how.”",
    teach=["Read 1 Peter 2:9. Say: “‘You are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, God’s own people, that you may declare the wonderful deeds of him who called you out of darkness into his marvelous light.’ In Baptism, we are united to Christ, who is priest, prophet and king. And we share in his three roles.”", "Say: “Priest: Jesus offered himself to the Father. We share in this by offering our lives to God: our prayers, work, joys and sufferings, especially at Mass. We are a priestly people, and we sanctify the world. Prophet: Jesus spoke God’s truth. We share in this by witnessing to the truth by our words and our example, in our families, school and work. King: Jesus served as a king who washed feet. We share in this by serving others, and by ruling ourselves, mastering our own sin.”", "Say: “Some people have a special vocation as priests or religious. But all of us have a role. The laity are the Church’s presence in the world, in families, in business, in schools, in politics, in the arts. The Second Vatican Council taught that all Christians are called to holiness. Your Baptism is not a past event but a mission. Think about what yours is.”"],
    ask=["What three roles of Christ do we share? (Priest, prophet and king.)", "How do we share in the priesthood of Christ? (Offering our lives to God, prayer and Mass.)", "What is a way to be a prophet at school? (Tell the truth, defend someone, live by faith.)"],
    practice=["Fold a card in three parts: priest, prophet, king.", "In each, write one specific promise. For example: Priest: “I will pray the Morning Offering every day.” Prophet: “I will not join in gossip.” King: “I will help with the dishes without being asked.”", "Decorate the card with a cross and water drops (baptism).", "Share one with a partner and ask for accountability."],
    close="Renew your baptismal promises. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Put your card where you will see it every day.”")),

'5-3-1': dict(
  saint="Peter Julian Eymard: a priest who spent his life spreading love for the Eucharist.",
  tryit=["Draw the Eucharist in the middle.", "Draw arrows from the other sacraments.", "Explain why it is the source and summit."],
  saintStory="Peter Julian Eymard was born in France in 1811. As a boy, he loved to stay in the church to pray in front of the tabernacle. He was so small that the sacristan sometimes found him on tiptoe to look at it.\nHe became a priest and joined the Marists, but he felt called to give his whole life to the Eucharist. In 1856 he started a new congregation, the Congregation of the Blessed Sacrament, whose members spend time each day adoring Jesus in the Eucharist and encouraging others to do the same. He also started a group for women, and a group of lay people to keep the Eucharistic fire alive.\nHe is called “the Apostle of the Eucharist.” He died in 1868. Peter Julian shows that the Eucharist is the center of Christian life.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a circle on the board and ask, “What is the center of a wheel? Without a center, nothing holds together. Is there a center in the Church’s life?”",
    teach=["Read John 6:35. Say: “Jesus said, ‘I am the bread of life; whoever comes to me shall not hunger, and whoever believes in me shall never thirst.’ He said this after he fed five thousand people with five loaves and two fish. Then he said that the bread he would give is his flesh, for the life of the world. The Eucharist is the fulfillment of that promise.”", "Say: “The Second Vatican Council called the Eucharist ‘the source and summit of the Christian life.’ Source means everything flows from it, like a spring. Summit means everything leads toward it, like the top of a mountain. All the other sacraments, all ministries and works of the Church are connected to it, and directed to it, because in the Eucharist we have Christ himself, our Pasch (Passover).”", "Say: “Think of it this way. Baptism brings us to the table. Confirmation strengthens us for the journey. Reconciliation cleans us so we can receive worthily. The Anointing of the Sick prepares us for the final journey. Marriage and Holy Orders serve the family of the Church that gathers. And every Mass sends us out to love. When we celebrate the Eucharist, we unite ourselves to Christ’s sacrifice on the cross, and with the whole Church.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘source and summit’ mean? (Everything flows from it and leads to it.)", "What did Jesus say about himself in John 6? (I am the bread of life.)", "How are other sacraments connected to the Eucharist? (They prepare for, flow from or lead to it.)"],
    practice=["Draw a large circle with the Eucharist at the center.", "Around it, put the other six sacraments. Draw arrows to or from each, and write how it relates to the Eucharist.", "Add one arrow for “mission,” going out from the Eucharist into the world.", "Explain your diagram to a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, you are the bread of life. We believe in you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Spend five minutes before the tabernacle if you can.”")),

'5-3-2': dict(
  saint="Juliana of Liège: a nun who asked for a feast to honor the Body and Blood of Christ.",
  tryit=["Review the evidence for the Real Presence.", "Write what each witness says.", "State what the Church believes."],
  saintStory="Juliana was born around 1193 near Liège, in what is now Belgium. She became an orphan when she was five and was raised by nuns. When she was sixteen, she began to have a vision of a bright moon with a dark spot. She came to understand that the moon was the Church’s year, and the dark spot was the absence of a feast in honor of the Blessed Sacrament.\nFor many years she told people about this. Eventually the bishop of Liège in 1246 established the feast in his diocese. In 1263, a priest in Bolsena, Italy, who found it hard to believe in the Real Presence, was said to have seen blood dripping from the Host during Mass. The next year Pope Urban IV declared the feast of Corpus Christi for the whole Church. He asked St. Thomas Aquinas to write the prayers and hymns for it.\nJuliana died in 1258, before the feast became universal. She helped the Church celebrate the Real Presence.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a loaf of bread. Say, “What is this? And after a priest says the words of consecration, what is it? If I told you that something looks and tastes like bread but is Jesus himself, how would you check?”",
    teach=["Read John 6:51-58 in pieces. Say: “Jesus said, ‘I am the living bread that came down from heaven. If anyone eats of this bread, he will live forever. And the bread that I will give for the life of the world is my flesh.’ The people argued, ‘How can this man give us his flesh to eat?’ Jesus did not take it back. He said it again, even more strongly: ‘Unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink his blood, you have no life in you.’ Many left him. He did not call them back to say it was a symbol.”", "Say: “At the Last Supper, Jesus took bread and said, ‘This is my body,’ and the cup, ‘This is my blood.’ (Luke 22:19-20) Paul wrote, ‘The cup of blessing which we bless, is it not a participation in the blood of Christ? The bread which we break, is it not a participation in the body of Christ?’ (1 Corinthians 10:16) The early Fathers, such as Ignatius of Antioch and Justin Martyr, said the same: it is not common bread.”", "Say: “The Church teaches that in the Eucharist, the whole Christ is present: Body, Blood, Soul and Divinity. This is called the Real Presence. The word transubstantiation means that the substance, what the thing really is, changes into Christ’s Body and Blood, while the appearances, or accidents, of bread and wine remain. This is a miracle, not an object of science. We believe because Christ said so, and the Church has always taught it. And we adore him in the Blessed Sacrament.”"],
    ask=["What does the Real Presence mean? (Jesus is truly, really and substantially present in the Eucharist.)", "What is transubstantiation? (The substance of bread and wine changes into Christ’s Body and Blood; appearances remain.)", "What do we do before the Blessed Sacrament? (Adore, genuflect, pray.)"],
    practice=["Set up a “witness file.” In teams, review evidence from Scripture (John 6:51-58, Luke 22:19-20, 1 Corinthians 11:23-29), the early Church (Ignatius and Justin), and the Church’s teaching.", "Each team writes a one-paragraph summary: What does this witness say?", "Teams present their evidence in a short “trial” where one student asks questions and others answer.", "Write a one-sentence statement of the Church’s faith."],
    close="Pray the Adoro Te or: “Jesus, I adore you in the Blessed Sacrament.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Genuflect with reverence at Mass.”")),

'5-3-3': dict(
  saint="Dominic Savio: a boy who prepared for his First Communion with promises he kept.",
  tryit=["Write a prayer before Communion.", "Write a prayer after Communion.", "Say what you need to be ready."],
  saintStory="Dominic Savio received his First Communion at the age of seven, in 1849, in a village near Turin, Italy. In those days, children usually had to wait until they were older, but Dominic’s priest saw how much he loved Jesus. Dominic prepared with great care. He wrote four promises for the day: “One: I will go to Confession and Communion as often as my confessor permits. Two: I want to sanctify Sundays and feast days. Three: My friends will be Jesus and Mary. Four: Death rather than sin.”\nHe kept these promises throughout his short life. Dominic also went to Confession regularly, and he made time each day to thank Jesus after Communion. At twelve he met Don Bosco, and joined his school in Turin, and showed his friends how to be good. He died at fourteen.\nDominic shows how to prepare for Communion, and how to give thanks.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “If the President of the country was coming to your house for dinner, what would you do to get ready? Clean, put on nice clothes, think about what to say. Now we are going to receive someone even greater. How should we prepare?”",
    teach=["Read 1 Corinthians 11:27-29. Say: “Paul warns the Christians in Corinth: ‘Whoever eats the bread or drinks the cup of the Lord in an unworthy manner will be guilty of profaning the body and blood of the Lord. Let a man examine himself, and so eat of the bread and drink of the cup.’ This is serious: we are receiving Jesus himself.”", "Say: “The Church asks us to prepare in four ways. One: believe what the Church teaches about the Eucharist. Two: be in the state of grace. This means we must not have unconfessed mortal sin on our soul. If we are aware of one, we must go to Confession first. Three: fast for at least one hour before Communion from food and drink (water and medicine are allowed). Four: come with reverence, dressed and acting respectfully, with a quiet heart. Genuflect, fold hands, receive with devotion, and say ‘Amen’ with faith.”", "Say: “After Communion we give thanks. Jesus is really within us. This is a precious time for silent prayer. We can say, ‘Thank you, Jesus, I love you, help me.’ The Church encourages us to receive Communion often, and at least once a year during the Easter season. We also make a spiritual communion when we cannot receive, asking Jesus to come into our hearts. The fruits of Communion: closer union with Christ, forgiveness of venial sins, protection from mortal sin, unity with the Church, and love for the poor.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘state of grace’ mean? (Free from mortal sin, in God’s friendship.)", "How long should we fast before Communion? (One hour.)", "What do we do after Communion? (Give thanks, pray quietly.)"],
    practice=["Fold a card in two. On the left side, write a prayer to say before Communion in your own words (for example, “Jesus, I believe you are here. Make my heart clean.”).", "On the right side, write a prayer for after Communion (“Thank you, Jesus, for coming to me. Help me love others.”).", "Decorate with a chalice and host.", "Read your prayers quietly to yourself."],
    close="Pray together the Act of Spiritual Communion: “My Jesus, I believe that you are present in the Blessed Sacrament.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Use your prayer cards at Mass.”")),

'5-4-1': dict(
  saint="Ezra the scribe: the priest who read God’s word to the people, and who wept for joy.",
  tryit=["Follow the readings with a missal.", "Mark the parts of the Introductory Rites.", "Write one thing God says to you."],
  saintStory="After the people of Israel returned from exile in Babylon, they rebuilt the walls of Jerusalem. Then, in a great public gathering, Ezra the priest and scribe stood on a wooden platform and read the book of the Law of Moses to them, from early morning until midday.\nThe Levites helped, explaining the meaning so that all could understand. As people listened, they began to weep, because they realized how much they had neglected God’s word. But Nehemiah and Ezra said to them, “This day is holy to the Lord your God. Do not mourn or weep… Do not be grieved, for the joy of the Lord is your strength.” So they went off to eat and drink and celebrate, because they had understood the words.\nThis scene is a picture of the Liturgy of the Word at Mass: the word is read, explained, and received with joy.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read a short passage aloud in a very dull, quick voice. Then read the same passage with feeling. Ask, “What is the difference? When God’s word is read well, it can change us.”",
    teach=["Read Nehemiah 8:1-12 in summary. Say: “Ezra read from the book of the Law, all morning. The people listened. The priests explained it. The people wept, and then they rejoiced. This is a picture of the first main part of Mass, the Liturgy of the Word. Jesus says, ‘He who hears you hears me.’ In the readings God speaks to his people.”", "Say: “The Mass begins with the Introductory Rites. We gather, sing the entrance song, and the priest greets us. We make the Sign of the Cross. We ask forgiveness in the Penitential Act. We sing ‘Lord, have mercy’ and, on most Sundays, the ‘Glory to God’. And the priest says the Collect, or opening prayer. These rites help us to become a community ready to listen.”", "Say: “Then comes the Liturgy of the Word. The first reading is usually from the Old Testament. The responsorial psalm is sung. The second reading is from the New Testament letters. We stand and sing the Gospel Acclamation (the Alleluia). The Gospel is read by the priest or deacon. The homily explains the readings. On Sundays we say the Creed, and we pray the Prayer of the Faithful for the needs of the Church and the world. Over three years, the Sunday readings cover most of the Bible.”"],
    ask=["What are the parts of the Introductory Rites? (Entrance, greeting, Penitential Act, Gloria, Collect.)", "What are the readings on Sunday? (First reading, psalm, second reading, Gospel.)", "Why do we stand for the Gospel? (It is Christ’s own words.)"],
    practice=["Give each student a copy of the Sunday readings (or a missal).", "Read the Gospel aloud together, and then the first reading.", "Each student writes: one word or phrase that stood out, one question, and one thing God might be saying to me today.", "Share in pairs."],
    close="Pray: “Speak, Lord, your servant is listening.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read the Sunday Gospel before Mass.”")),

'5-4-2': dict(
  saint="Isaiah: the prophet who saw heaven and heard, “Holy, holy, holy.”",
  tryit=["Put the steps of the Liturgy of the Eucharist in order.", "Race with your team.", "Explain the central moment."],
  saintStory="Isaiah was a prophet in Jerusalem about 700 years before Christ. In the year that King Uzziah died, he was in the Temple and had a vision. He saw the Lord sitting on a high throne, and the hem of his robe filled the Temple. Angels called seraphim hovered above, each with six wings, crying to each other, “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord of hosts; the whole earth is full of his glory!”\nThe doorposts shook, and the house filled with smoke. Isaiah cried, “Woe is me! I am a man of unclean lips.” One of the seraphim flew to him with a burning coal from the altar and touched his mouth, saying, “Your guilt is taken away, and your sin forgiven.” Then he heard the voice of the Lord: “Whom shall I send?” and Isaiah said, “Here I am! Send me.”\nAt every Mass, in the Eucharistic Prayer, we sing Isaiah’s words: “Holy, holy, holy.” We join the angels as Jesus comes to the altar.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read Isaiah 6:1-3 dramatically. Say, “Imagine being there. This is what we join every time we say ‘Holy, holy, holy’ at Mass.”",
    teach=["Say: “The second main part of the Mass is the Liturgy of the Eucharist. It has three parts. First, the Preparation of the Gifts: bread and wine are brought to the altar, with the collection for the poor. The priest offers them to God. Second, the Eucharistic Prayer, the heart of the Mass. It begins with the Preface, a prayer of thanks. We sing the Holy, Holy, Holy (the Sanctus). The priest asks the Holy Spirit to come upon the gifts (the epiclesis). Then comes the consecration.”", "Read Luke 22:19. Say: “At the consecration, the priest says the words of Jesus: ‘Take this, all of you, and eat of it, for this is my Body, which will be given up for you.’ And over the chalice: ‘Take this, all of you, and drink from it, for this is the chalice of my Blood…’ At that moment the bread and wine become Jesus’ Body and Blood. The priest lifts the Host and the chalice, and we adore. The Eucharistic Prayer continues with remembering Christ’s death and Resurrection, praying for the Church, the living and the dead.”", "Say: “Third, the Communion Rite. We pray the Our Father together. We offer each other the Sign of Peace. The priest breaks the Host while we sing the Lamb of God. We say, ‘Lord, I am not worthy that you should enter under my roof, but only say the word and my soul shall be healed.’ Then we receive Holy Communion. We then pray quietly and the priest says the Prayer after Communion.”"],
    ask=["What happens at the consecration? (Bread and wine become Christ’s Body and Blood.)", "What do we sing in the Eucharistic Prayer? (Holy, Holy, Holy.)", "What do we say before Communion? (Lord, I am not worthy…)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards: Preparation of the gifts, Preface, Sanctus, Epiclesis, Consecration, Memorial acclamation, Great Amen, Our Father, Sign of Peace, Lamb of God, Communion, Prayer after Communion.", "Teams race to put the cards in order.", "Check the order with a missal.", "Choose three cards and explain what they mean."],
    close="Pray the Holy, Holy, Holy together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “At Mass, notice when Isaiah’s words are sung.”")),

'5-4-3': dict(
  tryit=["Choose a service project as a class.", "Plan how to do it.", "Do it and report back."],
  saintStory="Agnes Gonxha Bojaxhiu was born in 1910, in what is now North Macedonia. She became a Sister of Loreto and taught in Calcutta, India, for nearly twenty years. In 1946, on a train, she felt Jesus call her to serve the poorest of the poor. She left the convent, put on a plain white sari with blue stripes, and went out into the streets of Calcutta.\nShe started the Missionaries of Charity in 1950. Every day began with Mass and prayer. The sisters spent an hour in adoration before the Blessed Sacrament, and then went out to care for the sick, the dying, the unwanted and the abandoned. Mother Teresa said, “In the Eucharist I see Jesus under the appearance of bread. In the slums I see him in the distressing disguise of the poor.”\nShe died in 1997 and was named a saint in 2016. Mother Teresa shows that the Eucharist sends us out to serve.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask the children, “Have you ever got a really good gift and wanted to tell everybody about it?” Take a few answers. “At Mass, we receive the best gift in the world. At the end, the priest tells us what to do with it.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Mass ends with the Concluding Rites. The priest greets us again, and gives a blessing, usually in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit. Then the deacon or priest sends us out with one of these: ‘Go forth, the Mass is ended,’ or ‘Go in peace, glorifying the Lord by your life,’ or ‘Go in peace.’ We answer, ‘Thanks be to God.’”", "Say: “The Latin words are ‘Ite, missa est,’ which literally mean ‘Go, it is the sending.’ The word ‘Mass’ comes from missa, ‘sent.’ So the very name Mass tells us that we are being sent. In Mark 16:15, Jesus told the apostles, ‘Go into all the world and preach the Gospel to the whole creation.’ We too are sent to bring Christ to the world.”", "Say: “What we receive at Mass is meant to be given away. We were fed so that we can feed. We were forgiven so that we can forgive. We heard the Word so that we can share it. Jesus said, ‘Whatever you did for one of the least of these my brothers, you did for me.’ The Eucharist and love for the poor go together. The Mass doesn’t end when we leave the church. It continues in how we live the rest of the week.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘Ite, missa est’ mean? (Go, you are sent.)", "Where does the word ‘Mass’ come from? (From missa, ‘sent.’)", "How can we carry the Eucharist into our week? (Serve others, share, forgive.)"],
    practice=["As a class, brainstorm service projects: writing cards for people in a nursing home, collecting food, cleaning a parish area, praying for the sick.", "Choose one and plan it: who, what, when, and how.", "Make or gather what you need.", "Carry it out, and reflect on how it connects to the Mass."],
    close="Say together the dismissal: “Go in peace, glorifying the Lord by your life.” Answer: “Thanks be to God.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one act of service this week as your ‘mission.’”")),

'5-5-1': dict(
  saint="Zacchaeus: the tax collector who climbed a tree to see Jesus and gave back what he had taken.",
  tryit=["Act out Zacchaeus in four frames.", "Show how he changed.", "Say what real change looks like."],
  saintStory="Zacchaeus was the chief tax collector in Jericho, and he was rich. Tax collectors worked for the Romans, and they usually took extra for themselves, so people did not like them. Zacchaeus was also short.\nWhen Jesus came to Jericho, a big crowd lined the streets, and Zacchaeus could not see. So he ran ahead and climbed a sycamore tree. When Jesus came to the tree, he looked up and said, “Zacchaeus, hurry and come down, for I must stay at your house today.” Zacchaeus came down at once and welcomed him joyfully. The crowd muttered that Jesus was staying with a sinner.\nZacchaeus said, “Behold, Lord, the half of my goods I give to the poor; and if I have defrauded anyone of anything, I restore it fourfold.” Jesus said, “Today salvation has come to this house.” An old tradition says that Zacchaeus later became a bishop. He shows what true conversion is: a change of heart that shows in what we do.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask a child to quickly stand on a chair. “What can you see from here that you could not see before? Zacchaeus climbed a tree for a good view. Let’s see what he saw.”",
    teach=["Read Luke 19:1-10 aloud. Say: “Zacchaeus was curious. He wanted to see Jesus. But Jesus saw him first, and called him by name. That is how it works with God: he looks for us first.”", "Say: “What is sin? Sin is an offense against God, against reason, and against truth. It is a failure in genuine love for God and neighbor. It wounds our nature and harms human solidarity. Every sin damages our relationship with God, with others and with ourselves. Some sins are done, some left undone. They can be in thought, word, deed and omission. We cannot just ignore it.”", "Say: “But God calls us to conversion, which means turning around. Conversion starts with God’s grace, as we see with Zacchaeus. Jesus does not wait until he has changed to love him. Jesus’ love changes him. And real conversion shows itself in action: Zacchaeus gave to the poor and paid back what he took. Penance is not just a feeling of sadness. It is a change of life.”"],
    ask=["What is sin? (An offense against God, reason and truth; a failure in love.)", "What is conversion? (Turning the heart back to God.)", "What did Zacchaeus do to show he had changed? (Gave to the poor, paid back four times.)"],
    practice=["Choose students to play Zacchaeus, Jesus, the crowd and the poor.", "Act out the story in four frames: the tree, the call, the dinner, the change.", "In each frame, freeze, and ask the actors what they’re thinking.", "Write one sentence about what real change looks like, and share."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, help me to turn to you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Is there something you need to make right? Do it this week.”")),

'5-5-2': dict(
  saint="Padre Pio: a friar who heard confessions for many hours every day.",
  tryit=["Walk through the stations of Confession.", "Say what you do at each.", "Practice the Act of Contrition."],
  saintStory="Francesco Forgione was born in Italy in 1887. He became a Capuchin friar and took the name Pio. In 1916 he went to live at a friary in San Giovanni Rotondo, a small town in the mountains, and stayed there for over fifty years.\nIn 1918 people began to say that he had the wounds of Christ, the stigmata, on his hands, feet and side. This brought huge crowds to see him. But Padre Pio gave most of his time to something else: hearing confessions. He sat in the confessional for ten, twelve, even fourteen hours a day. People waited days to see him. Many said that he seemed to know their sins before they told him, and that they came out feeling a great peace.\nHe also said Mass with great reverence and started a hospital, the House for the Relief of Suffering. He died in 1968. Padre Pio shows what the sacrament of Penance is: mercy given through a priest.",
  script=dict(
    open="Have two cups: one clean, one muddy. Say, “Imagine drinking out of these. Which would you choose? Sin makes our soul like the muddy cup. The sacrament of Penance cleans it.”",
    teach=["Read John 20:23. Say: “On Easter evening, Jesus breathed on the apostles and said, ‘If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven; if you retain the sins of any, they are retained.’ The Church has always understood that this power continued with bishops and priests. In Penance, a priest acts in the person of Christ, the Head. That is why we confess our sins to a priest, and not only to God.”", "Say: “The sacrament has four parts. One: contrition: sorrow for our sins and a firm purpose not to sin again. Perfect contrition comes from love of God. Imperfect contrition comes from fear of punishment or the ugliness of sin, and it is enough for the sacrament. Two: confession: telling our sins to the priest. We must confess all mortal sins we remember, in kind and number. It’s also good to confess venial sins. Three: satisfaction, or penance: the priest gives us a prayer or act to repair the harm and to heal. Four: absolution: the priest says, ‘I absolve you from your sins in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.’”", "Say: “The priest is bound by the seal of confession. He can never reveal what he has heard. Nothing, not the law, not any person, can make him. Priests have died rather than break it. Why Confession? Because it gives us certainty that we are forgiven, helps us to name our sins honestly, and restores us to the Church. It also brings peace. Pope Francis called it ‘a hug from God.’ We should go regularly, and especially before we receive Communion with a mortal sin.”"],
    ask=["What are the four parts of Penance? (Contrition, confession, satisfaction, absolution.)", "Why do we go to a priest? (He acts in the person of Christ, as Jesus gave the apostles the power.)", "What is the seal of confession? (The priest may never reveal what he hears.)"],
    practice=["Set up stations around the room: Examine your conscience (a list of questions), Be sorry (the Act of Contrition), Confess (a mock confessional with two chairs), Receive penance (a card), Absolution (the words of absolution).", "Walk through each station, one at a time.", "At the “confessional,” practice the opening words: “Bless me, Father, for I have sinned. It has been ___ since my last confession.”", "Say the Act of Contrition aloud together."],
    close="Pray the Act of Contrition. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Plan to go to Confession as a family.”")),

'5-5-3': dict(
  saint="Marianne Cope: a sister who cared for people with leprosy on Molokai.",
  tryit=["Write cards to the sick.", "Practice words of comfort.", "Pray for them."],
  saintStory="Barbara Cope was born in Germany in 1838 and moved with her family to Utica, New York, when she was a baby. She worked in a factory to help her family until she was old enough to join the Sisters of St. Francis in Syracuse, where she took the name Marianne. She became a teacher, a nurse, and then the head of her community. She helped start two of the first hospitals in central New York.\nIn 1883, she received a letter from the king of Hawaii, asking for sisters to care for people with leprosy, a disease from which many people shrank in fear. Many other communities had refused. Mother Marianne wrote back, “I am hungry for the work… I will cheerfully accept the work.” She went with six other sisters to Hawaii, where she lived for thirty-five years, caring for patients on the island of Molokai. She cared for Father Damien when he was dying.\nMarianne died in 1918, and was named a saint in 2012. She shows how love can reach the sick, and why the Church anoints them.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Have you ever been sick for a long time, or visited someone who was? What helped? Did you wish for something to make you feel better?” Take answers. “God has a sacrament for exactly this.”",
    teach=["Read James 5:14-15. Say: “‘Is anyone among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the Church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord; and the prayer of faith will save the sick man, and the Lord will raise him up; and if he has committed sins, he will be forgiven.’ From the first days of the Church, Christians have prayed for their sick and anointed them with oil.”", "Say: “The Anointing of the Sick is given to someone who is seriously ill, or facing surgery, or growing weak from old age. It can be received more than once, if the illness gets worse or returns. The priest lays hands on the person, anoints the forehead and hands with the oil of the sick, and prays: ‘Through this holy anointing may the Lord in his love and mercy help you with the grace of the Holy Spirit. May the Lord who frees you from sin save you and raise you up.’”", "Say: “What does it do? It gives strength, peace and courage to face illness or old age. It unites the person to Christ’s suffering. It forgives sins if the person could not go to Confession. It sometimes brings healing of the body, if it is God’s will, but the main healing is of the soul. It also prepares a person for the final journey. When someone is dying, the Church also gives Viaticum, which means ‘food for the journey,’ the Eucharist received for the last time. The sacrament is not only for the dying. It is for anyone who needs strength.”"],
    ask=["Who can receive the Anointing of the Sick? (Anyone seriously ill, or weak from old age.)", "What does the priest anoint? (Forehead and hands.)", "What is Viaticum? (The Eucharist received at the end of life: food for the journey.)"],
    practice=["Give each student a card and markers.", "Write a message to someone who is ill or lonely (a grandparent, a neighbor, someone in a hospital or nursing home).", "Practice kind, simple words of comfort: “I am thinking of you. I am praying for you.”", "Pray a decade of the Rosary or an Our Father for the sick as a group, then deliver the cards."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, bless and heal the sick.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Visit or call someone who is sick.”")),

'5-6-1': dict(
  saint="Michael McGivney: a parish priest who started the Knights of Columbus to help families.",
  tryit=["Prepare questions for a priest or deacon.", "Interview them.", "Write what surprised you."],
  saintStory="Michael McGivney was born in 1852 in Waterbury, Connecticut, the oldest of thirteen children, to Irish immigrants. He left school at thirteen to work in a factory to help his family, but later went to seminary, and was ordained in 1877. He served at St. Mary’s Church in New Haven.\nHe saw that when a father in a parish died, the family often fell into poverty. Many Catholic men also joined secret societies that were against the Church for the insurance benefits. In 1882 he gathered a group of men in the church basement and started the Knights of Columbus, a Catholic fraternal organization to help families, strengthen faith and care for widows and orphans.\nFather McGivney died of pneumonia in 1890, at thirty-eight. He was beatified in 2020. He shows what a priest does: serve, listen, and build up his people.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you could ask a priest or a deacon any question, what would it be?” Write the questions on the board. Say, “We’ll use these in an interview.”",
    teach=["Say: “Holy Orders is the sacrament in which Christ continues his own priesthood through his chosen ministers. It has three degrees. Bishops are the successors of the apostles. They lead a diocese, ordain priests and deacons, and confirm. Priests are the bishop’s co-workers. They preach, celebrate Mass, hear confessions and anoint the sick. Deacons assist at Mass, proclaim the Gospel, preach, baptize, witness marriages and serve the poor.”", "Read Luke 22:19: “Do this in memory of me.” Say: “At the Last Supper, Jesus gave the apostles the command to do what he had done. The apostles passed on their ministry by the laying on of hands to others. This unbroken chain is called apostolic succession. In the sacrament of Holy Orders, the bishop lays hands on the man and prays for the Holy Spirit. The man is changed: he is marked forever. A priest acts ‘in persona Christi capitis’, in the person of Christ the Head.”", "Say: “Only baptized men are ordained, following Jesus’ choice of the Twelve and the Church’s constant tradition. Most priests in the Latin Church promise celibacy, which means not marrying, to give themselves entirely to Christ and the Church. Eastern Catholic churches can ordain married men to the priesthood. Every priest was first a boy in a parish like ours. We pray for vocations. And we respect and thank our priests and deacons.”"],
    ask=["What are the three degrees of Holy Orders? (Bishop, priest, deacon.)", "What does a priest do? (Celebrates Mass, hears confessions, anoints the sick, preaches.)", "What does ‘in the person of Christ’ mean? (The priest acts as Christ acts through him.)"],
    practice=["Invite a priest or a deacon to answer student questions, or watch a short interview video.", "Beforehand, work together to choose the five best questions: How did you know you were called? What is your favorite part of the day?", "Take notes during the interview.", "Afterward, each student writes one thing that surprised them and one thing they’ll pray for the priest."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, bless our priests, deacons and bishops.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Thank a priest or deacon this week.”")),

'5-6-2': dict(
  saint="Priscilla and Aquila: a married couple who worked with Paul and opened their home to the Church.",
  tryit=["Walk through the wedding rite.", "Say the vows in your own words.", "Say what they promise."],
  saintStory="Priscilla and Aquila were a Jewish couple who lived in Rome, and then in Corinth, Ephesus and Rome again. They were tentmakers by trade, as was Paul, so when Paul came to Corinth, he stayed with them and worked alongside them.\nThey became close friends and co-workers of Paul. They traveled with him to Ephesus, and when a gifted preacher named Apollos came who knew only part of the Gospel, Priscilla and Aquila took him aside and explained the way of God more accurately. Paul wrote in his letter to the Romans, “Greet Prisca and Aquila, my fellow workers in Christ Jesus, who risked their necks for my life.” He added, “and the church in their house.”\nTheir marriage was a partnership in faith, work and ministry. Priscilla and Aquila show how marriage is a vocation to serve God together.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What is a wedding? What happens? Why do people make promises on that day?” Write answers on the board. “Marriage is a sacrament, and a sign of God’s love.”",
    teach=["Read Genesis 2:18-24. Say: “God said, ‘It is not good that the man should be alone.’ He made a woman, and the man said, ‘This at last is bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh.’ And the text says, ‘Therefore a man leaves his father and his mother and cleaves to his wife, and they become one flesh.’ Marriage is God’s idea. It is a covenant, a sacred promise that makes them a family.”", "Say: “In the sacrament of Matrimony, a baptized man and woman make a covenant for life. By their free consent, they give themselves to each other. They promise to be faithful, to love each other for life, and to be open to children. Marriage is ordered to the good of the spouses and to the procreation and education of children. It is a sign of Christ’s love for his Church: faithful, free, total and fruitful. Paul wrote about this in Ephesians 5.”", "Say: “In the Latin Church, the bride and groom are the ministers of the sacrament. The priest or deacon is the Church’s official witness. The rite includes the entrance, the readings, the questions, the exchange of consent, the blessing and exchange of rings, the nuptial blessing, and often a Mass. The couple promise to take each other ‘for better, for worse, for richer, for poorer, in sickness and in health, until death do us part.’ Marriage is a lifelong, faithful and fruitful union. God’s grace helps the couple become holy together.”"],
    ask=["What is marriage? (A covenant between a baptized man and woman, for life.)", "What do the spouses promise? (Faithfulness, love for life, openness to children.)", "What does marriage signify? (Christ’s love for the Church.)"],
    practice=["Walk through the wedding rite on cards: entrance, readings, questions, exchange of consent, blessing and exchange of rings, nuptial blessing, Mass.", "Students place them in order and say what each means.", "In pairs, rewrite the vows in your own words, as a promise to a friend, family member or teammate.", "Share one."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, bless every married couple.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask your parents about their wedding.”")),

'5-6-3': dict(
  tryit=["Write a vocation prayer.", "Use Samuel’s words.", "Listen in silence."],
  saintStory="Samuel was a boy dedicated to God. His mother, Hannah, had prayed for a child, and she promised to give him to the Lord. So when he was small, he went to live in the sanctuary at Shiloh with the priest Eli. He wore a little linen ephod and helped in the temple.\nOne night, as he lay down to sleep, he heard a voice call his name. He ran to Eli, saying, “Here I am, for you called me.” Eli said he had not called. This happened three times. Then Eli realized that the Lord was calling the boy. He told Samuel, “Go and lie down, and if he calls you, you shall say, ‘Speak, Lord, for your servant hears.’” Samuel did, and God gave him a message.\nSamuel grew up to be a prophet and a judge, and he anointed the first two kings of Israel, Saul and David. He shows us what a vocation sounds like: God calls by name, and we listen.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What do you want to be when you grow up? Think of something. Now, what if God has a plan for you? What if he is calling you by name?”",
    teach=["Say: “Vocation comes from the Latin word for ‘call.’ Everyone has a vocation, a call from God. First, God calls us all to holiness, to love him and others and to be with him forever. That’s the call of Baptism. Then he calls each person to a particular way of living it. In 1 Samuel 3:1-10, God called Samuel in the night. Samuel didn’t recognize it at first. We too may not recognize God’s voice at first.”", "Say: “There are four main ways to live out our call. Married life: a man and woman who give themselves to each other and their children. Priesthood or diaconate: men who serve the Church through Holy Orders. Consecrated religious life: sisters, brothers and others who vow to live poverty, chastity and obedience for the Kingdom, in community. And dedicated single life: people who serve God and others without marrying, as lay missionaries, teachers, nurses, and so on. All are good, and all are ways to love.”", "Say: “How do we discern, or find out, our calling? We pray and listen. We study Scripture. We receive the sacraments, especially Reconciliation and the Eucharist. We ask advice from parents, priests and mentors. We look at our gifts and desires. We serve others. And we trust that God will give us what we need. We don’t have to know everything now. God’s call often comes step by step. You can say what Samuel said: ‘Speak, Lord, your servant is listening.’”"],
    ask=["What does ‘vocation’ mean? (A call.)", "What is the first call that everyone has? (To holiness.)", "How do we discern our vocation? (Prayer, Scripture, sacraments, advice, serving.)"],
    practice=["Light a candle and sit in a quiet circle.", "Read 1 Samuel 3:1-10 slowly.", "Each student writes a short prayer: “Speak, Lord, your servant is listening. Help me to hear you, and to follow where you lead me.”", "After three minutes of silence, share one phrase from a prayer."],
    close="Pray the vocation prayer together: “Speak, Lord, your servant is listening.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray each night: Lord, what do you want me to do?”")),

'5-7-1': dict(
  saint="Oscar Romero: an archbishop who spoke for the poor and was killed at Mass.",
  tryit=["Compare Old Law and New Law.", "See how Jesus fulfills each commandment.", "Pick a commandment to live more fully."],
  saintStory="Oscar Romero was born in 1917 in El Salvador, and became a priest, then a bishop. In 1977 he was named archbishop of San Salvador, the capital. At the time his country was torn by violence, and many poor people were being killed or taken away. At first Romero seemed quiet and cautious.\nThen, in 1977, a priest and friend, Rutilio Grande, was killed because he defended the poor. Romero changed. He started to speak out. Every Sunday he preached in the cathedral and his homilies were broadcast on the radio across the country. He named the victims, and called for an end to violence. He said to the soldiers, “No soldier is obliged to obey an order against the law of God.”\nOn March 24, 1980, while he was saying Mass in a hospital chapel, he was shot and killed. He was named a saint in 2018. Romero lived the New Law of love, even at the cost of his life.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write on the board two lists: “Don’t” and “Do.” Ask students to name some rules at home or school. Say, “Are rules only about what we should not do? Let’s see what Jesus says.”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 22:37-40. Say: “The greatest commandment is, ‘You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart… And you shall love your neighbor as yourself.’ This sums up the whole law. The Old Law is the law God gave Moses: the Ten Commandments and other rules. It is holy and good, but it could not give the grace to keep it.”", "Say: “Jesus gives the New Law, the law of love. He didn’t abolish the Old Law. He said, ‘I have not come to abolish the law but to fulfill it.’ (Matthew 5:17) In the Sermon on the Mount, he deepens it. The Old Law says, ‘You shall not kill.’ Jesus says, ‘Do not even be angry with your brother.’ The Old Law says, ‘You shall not commit adultery.’ Jesus says, ‘Do not look at someone with lust.’ The Old Law says, ‘An eye for an eye.’ Jesus says, ‘Love your enemies.’ The New Law goes to the heart.”", "Say: “And the New Law is different in another way: it is written on our hearts by the Holy Spirit. We are given the grace to live it. In Jeremiah 31:33, God promised, ‘I will put my law within them, and I will write it upon their hearts.’ The New Law is summed up in the Beatitudes and the Sermon on the Mount. It is also called the law of freedom, because the Spirit moves us to do what is good out of love, not just out of fear.”"],
    ask=["What are the two great commandments? (Love God and love your neighbor.)", "How is the New Law different from the Old? (It goes to the heart, and the Spirit gives grace to live it.)", "Where do we find the heart of the New Law? (The Sermon on the Mount and the Beatitudes.)"],
    practice=["Draw a two-column chart: Old Law and New Law.", "Fill in rows: “You shall not kill” vs. “Do not be angry; reconcile”; “Do not swear falsely” vs. “Let your yes be yes”; “An eye for an eye” vs. “Love your enemies.”", "Choose one row, and write what it looks like in daily life.", "Share a challenge."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, write your law on our hearts.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pick one New Law challenge this week.”")),

'5-7-2': dict(
  saint="Faustina Kowalska: a sister who spread trust in God’s mercy.",
  tryit=["Sort the examples with your teacher.", "Ask the three questions.", "Say what God’s mercy does."],
  saintStory="Helena Kowalska was born in Poland in 1905, the third of ten children in a poor farming family. She had only about three years of school and went to work as a housekeeper to help her family. At twenty, she entered a convent and became Sister Faustina.\nShe worked as a cook, a gardener and a porter. In 1931, she said Jesus appeared to her in a white robe with two rays of light shining from his heart, one red and one pale. He asked her to have an image painted with the words “Jesus, I trust in you.” He asked that the Sunday after Easter be a feast of mercy. Faustina wrote down what Jesus said in a diary. She died of tuberculosis in 1938, at thirty-three.\nPope John Paul II named her a saint in 2000, and established Divine Mercy Sunday. Faustina shows how to trust in God’s mercy, even when we have sinned.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you broke your friend’s toy by accident, versus on purpose after being told not to touch it, is that the same? Why not?” Take answers. “That helps us understand how sins differ.”",
    teach=["Read 1 John 5:16-17. Say: “John writes, ‘There is sin that is mortal and sin that is not.’ Sin is any thought, word, deed or omission against God’s law. Not all sins have the same weight. Venial sin weakens our love and harms our friendship with God, but doesn’t destroy it. Examples might be a small lie, being lazy, or an unkind word. We need forgiveness for these too, and they are forgiven in the Mass and prayer, and Confession is recommended.”", "Say: “Mortal sin is a grave break with God. It destroys love in our hearts and cuts us off from God’s life, which we call sanctifying grace. If we died in mortal sin without repentance, we would be separated from God forever. Three things must all be present for a sin to be mortal. One: grave matter. The act itself is seriously wrong, such as murder, adultery, serious theft or deliberately missing Sunday Mass. Two: full knowledge. We know it is seriously wrong. Three: deliberate consent. We freely choose it. If one is missing, the sin may be less serious.”", "Say: “Mortal sin can be forgiven through the sacrament of Reconciliation. If we have committed a mortal sin, we should go to Confession as soon as we can and not receive Communion until we have. But we should never despair. God’s mercy is greater than any sin. As the Lord told Faustina, ‘The greater the sinner, the greater the right he has to my mercy.’ We trust in the mercy of Jesus and do our best to avoid sin.”"],
    ask=["What are the three conditions for mortal sin? (Grave matter, full knowledge, deliberate consent.)", "What does venial sin do? (Weakens our love but does not destroy it.)", "What should we do after a mortal sin? (Go to Confession as soon as possible.)"],
    practice=["Prepare anonymous examples written on cards (for instance: “forgetting a prayer,” “cheating on a big test,” “missing Mass on purpose without reason,” “taking a pencil by mistake”).", "As a class, discuss them: Is the matter grave? Did the person know? Did they freely choose?", "Sort them into a spectrum: no sin, venial, possibly mortal.", "End by reading aloud: “Jesus, I trust in you.”"],
    close="Pray the Divine Mercy prayer: “Jesus, I trust in you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray ‘Jesus, I trust in you’ every day this week.”")),

'5-7-3': dict(
  saint="Lawrence of Rome: a deacon who said the poor were the Church’s treasure.",
  tryit=["Chart the 14 works of mercy.", "Add a local example for each.", "Choose one to do."],
  saintStory="Lawrence was one of seven deacons of the Church of Rome in the third century, when Pope Sixtus II led the Church. Deacons helped with the poor and managed the Church’s money and goods, which were used to feed hundreds of poor people in the city.\nIn the year 258 the emperor ordered that all Christian bishops, priests and deacons be killed. When Pope Sixtus was arrested, Lawrence followed him, weeping and saying he wanted to go with him. The pope told him that he would follow him in three days. In the meantime, the Roman prefect, who thought the Church was rich, ordered Lawrence to bring him the treasures of the Church.\nLawrence gathered the poor, the blind, the lame and the orphans, and presented them, saying, “These are the treasures of the Church.” He was put to death for his faith. Lawrence shows what the works of mercy mean: in the poor, we meet Christ.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a treasure chest. Ask, “What would you put in a treasure chest? What does the Church treasure most?” Take answers. “A saint answered this question in a very surprising way.”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 25:31-46 in summary. Say: “Jesus describes the Last Judgment. The King says to those on his right, ‘I was hungry and you gave me food, I was thirsty and you gave me drink, I was a stranger and you welcomed me, I was naked and you clothed me, I was sick and you visited me, I was in prison and you came to me.’ They ask, ‘When did we see you?’ He says, ‘As you did it to one of the least of these my brothers, you did it to me.’ Then to those on the left he says the opposite. We will be judged on love.”", "Say: “The Church has named seven corporal works of mercy, which help the body: feed the hungry, give drink to the thirsty, clothe the naked, shelter the homeless, visit the sick, visit the imprisoned, bury the dead. And seven spiritual works of mercy, which help the soul: instruct the ignorant, counsel the doubtful, admonish sinners, bear wrongs patiently, forgive offenses willingly, comfort the afflicted, pray for the living and the dead.”", "Say: “Mercy is love in action, especially for those in need. It means seeing the person in front of us as Jesus. We do not have to do all of them at once. We can start where we are: share lunch with someone who forgot theirs, invite someone who is alone, visit a grandparent, pray for people who are sad, forgive someone who has been unkind. The works of mercy are a way of living out our baptism.”"],
    ask=["How many corporal works of mercy are there? (Seven.) Spiritual? (Seven.)", "How will Jesus judge us? (On how we treated the least of his brothers and sisters.)", "What’s one work of mercy you can do this week? (Take answers.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart with the fourteen works of mercy in two columns.", "For each, write a local example: for instance, “Feed the hungry: donate to the food pantry.”", "As a class, choose one work to do together as a project this week.", "Plan: who will do what, and when."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, let me see you in the poor, and serve them.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one work of mercy and tell us about it.”")),

'5-8-1': dict(
  saint="Columba: an Irish monk who prayed the Psalms and brought the Gospel to Scotland.",
  tryit=["Pray Morning Prayer together.", "Take turns leading.", "Say what each part does."],
  saintStory="Columba, also known as Colm Cille, was born in Ireland in about 521, into a noble family. He studied under holy monks, and he founded several monasteries. He loved books and the psalms, and he spent many hours copying the Bible.\nAccording to an old story, he secretly copied a psalter belonging to another abbot, which led to a quarrel and a battle, in which many men died. Columba was deeply sorry, and as a penance he chose to leave Ireland and not see it again. In 563 he sailed to the island of Iona, off the coast of Scotland, with twelve companions.\nOn Iona, the monks prayed the psalms at set times of the day and night. From Iona, they carried the faith to Scotland and northern England. Columba died in 597, at the altar. His monastery shows how the Liturgy of the Hours turns the day into prayer.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ring a small bell or play a church bell recording. Ask, “What did bells tell people long ago? The hours of the day. Monks stopped what they were doing at certain times to pray. We still do it today.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church prays not only at Mass, but throughout the day. This prayer is called the Liturgy of the Hours, or the Divine Office. It is the daily prayer of the Church, said by priests, deacons, religious and many lay people. Psalm 119:164 says, ‘Seven times a day I praise you.’ And Paul writes, ‘Pray without ceasing.’”", "Say: “The two ‘hinges’ of the Hours are Morning Prayer (Lauds) and Evening Prayer (Vespers). Other hours include the Office of Readings, Daytime Prayer, and Night Prayer (Compline). Each hour is made of psalms, canticles, a short reading, intercessions and a closing prayer. The psalms are the main part. Jesus prayed them. The hours sanctify the day: in the morning we praise God for the new day, in the evening we thank him.”", "Say: “It’s a prayer of the whole Church, in union with Christ. Even when you pray it alone, you pray with everyone else who is praying it at that moment. Anyone can pray the Hours, and there are apps and books that make it easy. A simple pattern for Morning Prayer: an opening versicle (‘Lord, open my lips’ / ‘And my mouth will proclaim your praise’), a hymn, a psalm, a short reading, the Canticle of Zechariah (Benedictus), intercessions, the Our Father, and a closing prayer.”"],
    ask=["What is the Liturgy of the Hours? (The Church’s daily prayer.)", "What are the two hinges? (Morning Prayer and Evening Prayer.)", "What is the main part of each hour? (The psalms.)"],
    practice=["Gather students in two choirs facing each other.", "Pray a short Morning Prayer: the opening versicle, a hymn (sung or read), Psalm 63 (or another), a short Scripture reading, the Benedictus, intercessions, the Our Father, and a closing prayer.", "Alternate verses between the two choirs.", "Afterward, share what felt different about praying this way."],
    close="End with the Morning Prayer’s blessing. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray Evening Prayer with your family.”")),

'5-8-2': dict(
  saint="Leonard of Port Maurice: a friar who spread the Stations of the Cross.",
  tryit=["Pray the Stations of the Cross.", "Kneel or stand at each.", "Think about what Jesus did."],
  saintStory="Leonard was born in 1676 in the town of Porto Maurizio on the Italian coast. He joined the Franciscans and became a preacher. For many years he traveled through Italy, preaching missions: weeks of sermons, confession, and prayer in towns and villages. Huge crowds came, and many people changed their lives.\nLeonard loved the Stations of the Cross, a prayer in which we walk with Jesus along the path to Calvary. He set them up in more than 570 places. In 1750, the Pope asked him to put the Stations in the Colosseum in Rome, where many early Christians died for their faith.\nHe died in 1751, and is the patron of parish missions. Leonard shows how popular devotions like the Stations help us live the faith, and how they come from the liturgy.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a pilgrim path or a hiking trail. Ask, “Why do people walk to holy places? What do they learn on the way? We are going to take a walk, in prayer, along the way Jesus walked.”",
    teach=["Say: “Besides the liturgy, there are popular devotions. These are prayers and practices, such as the Rosary, the Stations of the Cross, Eucharistic Adoration, novenas (nine days of prayer), pilgrimages, processions and the veneration of saints. They are not required, but they help us to pray and express our faith in ways that fit our culture. They flow from the liturgy and lead back to it.”", "Say: “The Stations of the Cross started with pilgrims in Jerusalem who walked the Via Dolorosa, the way of sorrow, to follow Jesus’ steps to Calvary. When it became hard to travel, Christians in Europe set up stations in their own churches. There are fourteen: Jesus is condemned; takes up the cross; falls the first time; meets his mother; Simon helps him; Veronica wipes his face; he falls a second time; meets the women of Jerusalem; falls a third time; is stripped; is nailed to the cross; dies; is taken down; is laid in the tomb. Some add a fifteenth: the Resurrection.”", "Say: “At each station we say, ‘We adore you, O Christ, and we praise you, because by your holy cross you have redeemed the world.’ We think about Jesus’ love and what he suffered for us. We also see our own crosses. In Matthew 14:13-21, Jesus fed the crowd, and his compassion is the same as on the way to Calvary. The Stations are prayed especially on Fridays of Lent. Eucharistic Adoration is prayer before the Blessed Sacrament, where we simply remain with Jesus.”"],
    ask=["What is a devotion? (A popular prayer or practice that supports our faith.)", "How many Stations are there? (Fourteen.)", "What do we say at each station? (We adore you, O Christ, and we praise you…)"],
    practice=["Set up the fourteen Stations around the room with images.", "Walk from station to station together, kneeling or standing, silent, reading each station’s title and a short prayer.", "At each, say, “We adore you, O Christ…” and the response.", "At the end, sit in silence and write one thing you thought about."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, we thank you for your Cross.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray the Stations with your family during Lent.”")),

'5-8-3': dict(
  saint="Charles de Foucauld: a former soldier who lived a hidden life of prayer in the desert.",
  tryit=["Plan your day of prayer.", "Add a morning offering, a mealtime prayer and a bedtime prayer.", "Add short prayers for hard moments."],
  saintStory="Charles de Foucauld was born in 1858 in France. He became a soldier and an explorer, and for a time he lived without faith. In 1886, in a church in Paris, he knelt before a priest and made a confession. He later wrote, “As soon as I believed that there was a God, I understood that I could not do otherwise than live for him alone.”\nCharles became a Trappist monk, then lived as a servant in Nazareth, a quiet life of prayer and work. After being ordained a priest, he went to live in the Algerian Sahara among the Tuareg people. He built a small hermitage, spent hours in adoration, and made friends with his neighbors. He wanted to bring the Gospel by his presence, kindness and prayer.\nIn 1916 he was killed by a raiding party. He was named a saint in 2022. Charles shows that we can pray in any place, and offer all of our day to God.",
  script=dict(
    open="Set up two clocks. Ask, “How many minutes of the day do you spend praying? How many could you? Think of the day: waking up, walking to school, lunch, homework, evening. Where could God fit?”",
    teach=["Read 1 Thessalonians 5:16-18. Say: “‘Rejoice always, pray without ceasing, give thanks in all circumstances; for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus for you.’ How can we pray without ceasing? The Church teaches that we can’t be on our knees all the time, but we can make our whole life a prayer. We can offer everything we do to God.”", "Say: “Here are some ways. First, a Morning Offering: when we wake, we offer the day to God: ‘O Jesus, through the Immaculate Heart of Mary, I offer you my prayers, works, joys and sufferings of this day.’ Second, short prayers during the day: ‘Jesus, I trust in you,’ ‘Come, Holy Spirit,’ ‘Thank you, Lord,’ or the Jesus Prayer. We can say them while walking, waiting or working. Third, grace before and after meals. Fourth, an evening review: we thank God for the day, say sorry for our failings, and ask for help for tomorrow. Also: reading Scripture, praying the Rosary, going to Mass.”", "Say: “Why does it matter? Prayer is a relationship. A friendship needs regular time together. Without it, it fades. With it, it grows. Prayer in daily life helps us stay close to God, face challenges, and remember that God is with us. And if we miss a day or forget, we can start again. God is patient.”"],
    ask=["What is the Morning Offering? (A prayer offering the day to God.)", "What are some short prayers during the day? (Jesus, I trust in you; Come, Holy Spirit.)", "Why pray in daily life? (To stay close to God in a friendship.)"],
    practice=["Give each student a plan sheet divided into morning, mealtime, school, afternoon, evening, bedtime.", "In each part, write a short prayer: Morning Offering; Grace before meals; “Come, Holy Spirit” before homework; “Jesus, I trust in you” when worried; Examen at night.", "Add one new habit you will start this week.", "Share your plan with a partner, and pray the Morning Offering together."],
    close="Pray the Morning Offering together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Use your plan every day for a week.”")),

'5-9-1': dict(
  saint="Francis of Assisi: he wrote his own meditation on the Our Father.",
  tryit=["Illustrate “hallowed be thy name.”", "Illustrate “thy kingdom come.”", "Pray them slowly."],
  saintStory="Francis of Assisi loved the Our Father. He is said to have prayed it often. He also wrote a prayer called the Paraphrase of the Our Father, in which he went through each line and added words of praise, so that “Our Father” became “Most holy Father, our Creator, Redeemer, Consoler and Savior.”\nWhen he wrote about “hallowed be thy name,” he asked that “the knowledge of you may be made clearer in us.” For “thy kingdom come” he wrote, “that you may reign in us through grace and bring us to your kingdom, where we will see you clearly.” He prayed with great love for God’s glory.\nFrancis’s example shows that the first part of the Our Father is about God’s honor before our needs, and that praying it slowly, with love, can open our eyes.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask the students to say the Our Father out loud, quickly. Then say, “Now let’s try something. We’ll say only the first three lines, as slowly as we can, and think about each word.”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 6:9-10. Say: “Jesus taught us to pray: ‘Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name; thy kingdom come, thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven.’ Notice: before we ask for anything for ourselves, we first honor God. The first three petitions are about God’s glory.”", "Say: “‘Our Father.’ In Romans 8:15, Paul writes, ‘You have received the spirit of adoption, whereby we cry, Abba, Father.’ We call God Father because Jesus invites us to share in his relationship with the Father. We say ‘Our’ because we are not alone: we pray as part of a family. ‘Who art in heaven’ doesn’t mean far away. It means God is above all and everywhere, and also that our true home is with him. ‘Hallowed be thy name’ means ‘may your name be made holy.’ God’s name is already holy. We ask that it be honored in us and by all people.”", "Say: “‘Thy kingdom come’ means that we ask for the reign of God to spread, in our hearts and in the world, until Christ comes again. It’s the kingdom of justice, peace and love. This isn’t just waiting: we help build it by what we do. It also asks for the final coming of Christ. ‘Come, Lord Jesus.’ We begin with God’s name and God’s kingdom. When we put God first, everything else falls into place.”"],
    ask=["Why do we say ‘Our’ Father? (We pray together as a family.)", "What does ‘hallowed be thy name’ ask? (That God’s name be honored and treated as holy.)", "What does ‘thy kingdom come’ ask? (That God’s reign of love spread, and Christ return.)"],
    practice=["Fold a paper in half.", "On one side, write “Hallowed be thy name” and draw a picture of someone honoring God’s name: for instance, someone praying quietly, or a person saying grace.", "On the other, write “Thy kingdom come” and draw a picture of the world as God’s kingdom: peace, sharing, kindness.", "Pray the first three petitions slowly, one at a time, with a pause after each."],
    close="Pray the whole Our Father slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray the first three petitions every morning.”")),

'5-9-2': dict(
  saint="Paulinus of Nola: a rich man who gave everything away so the hungry could have bread.",
  tryit=["Make a “daily bread” collage.", "Show what we need each day.", "Add one thing you can share."],
  saintStory="Paulinus was born around 354 in what is now France, to a rich Roman family. He was educated, became a senator, and later governor. He married a wealthy Spanish woman named Therasia. They were baptized, and gradually they decided to give up their wealth and live a simple life.\nThey sold their lands and gave the money to the poor. They moved to Nola, near Naples in Italy, where Paulinus became a priest and later bishop. He built a church for pilgrims and a hostel for the poor, and he wrote letters and poems. An old story says that when a poor widow came, whose son was being held in slavery, and he had nothing left to give, Paulinus offered to take the boy’s place as a slave.\nHe died in 431. Paulinus lived the Our Father’s prayer for daily bread by sharing what he had.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a loaf of bread. Ask, “Why do we pray for ‘daily’ bread and not ‘a year’s worth’ of bread? Think about the story of manna in the desert: the Israelites could gather only what they needed each day.” Let students speculate.",
    teach=["Read Matthew 6:10-11. Say: “‘Thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven. Give us this day our daily bread.’ In heaven the angels do God’s will perfectly. We ask that it be done on earth too, and in us. This is not resignation. It means we ask God to teach us to want what he wants. Jesus prayed this way in Gethsemane: ‘Not my will, but yours be done.’”", "Say: “Then we ask for our daily bread. This means what we need to live: food, clothing, shelter, health, work. It reminds us that everything comes from God. We ask for ‘our’ bread, not just ‘my’ bread, which includes the hungry. We ask for ‘daily’ bread, meaning enough for today, trusting God for tomorrow. It teaches us to rely on God, and to share. If we have more than we need, we have a responsibility to the one who has less.”", "Read John 6:35: “I am the bread of life.” Say: “There’s another meaning too. Jesus is the bread of life. The Church Fathers saw ‘daily bread’ as also the Eucharist, which feeds our soul. So we ask for what we need in body and soul. We also work: Jesus told us to ask for bread, but we also do our part to earn it and to help others. And at Mass, we pray this prayer just before we receive Communion.”"],
    ask=["What do we ask for when we say ‘Thy will be done’? (That God’s will be done in us and everywhere.)", "What does ‘daily bread’ mean? (What we need for today, in body and soul.)", "Why do we say ‘our’ bread? (We pray for everyone, especially the hungry.)"],
    practice=["On a sheet of poster board, make a “daily bread” collage with magazine pictures or drawings of things we need each day: food, water, a home, family, health, school, friends.", "In the middle, draw bread.", "Around the edge, write one action to share your bread with someone in need.", "Present your collage to a partner."],
    close="Pray the third and fourth petitions together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Give something to someone who needs it this week.”")),

'5-9-3': dict(
  saint="John Gualbert: a nobleman who forgave his brother’s killer and became a monk.",
  tryit=["Role-play saying sorry and forgiving.", "Draw a shield for “deliver us from evil.”", "Pray the last petitions."],
  saintStory="John Gualbert was born around 985, in Florence, Italy, to a noble family. When his brother Hugh was killed in a quarrel, his father told John that he must take revenge.\nOne Good Friday, John was riding into Florence with armed men when he met the killer, alone on a narrow path, with no place to run. The man dropped to his knees with his arms stretched out in the shape of a cross, and begged for mercy in the name of Jesus, who died on Good Friday. John thought of Christ forgiving from the cross. He put away his sword, raised the man up and embraced him.\nHe went into a church nearby to pray, and, according to the story, the figure of Christ on the crucifix bowed its head to him. John became a monk and founded a new community at Vallombrosa. He died in 1073. John’s story is the Our Father in action: “Forgive us as we forgive.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Is it harder to say sorry or to forgive? Why?” Let students discuss in pairs. “Jesus put these two together in one prayer.”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 6:12-15. Say: “‘And forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.’ After the Our Father, Jesus explained one line: ‘If you forgive others their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you; but if you do not forgive others their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses.’ That is serious. We can’t receive God’s mercy while refusing to give it. It’s not a deal: it’s that our hearts must be open.”", "Say: “Forgiving doesn’t mean pretending it didn’t hurt. It doesn’t mean there are no consequences. It means choosing not to hold it against the person, to let go of the desire for revenge, and to wish them well. It’s hard, and sometimes it takes time and prayer. Jesus helps us do it. He forgave from the cross: ‘Father, forgive them.’”", "Say: “‘And lead us not into temptation.’ God doesn’t tempt us (James 1:13). We ask: ‘Don’t let us enter into temptation; keep us from giving in.’ Jesus said, ‘Watch and pray that you may not enter into temptation.’ (Matthew 26:41) ‘But deliver us from evil.’ We ask God to rescue us from the evil one, Satan, and from all evil. The Our Father ends with trust: Our Father protects us. At Mass we add: ‘Deliver us, Lord, from every evil, graciously grant peace in our days…’ and then the doxology: ‘For the kingdom, the power and the glory are yours.’”"],
    ask=["Why must we forgive? (Jesus said God forgives us as we forgive others.)", "Does forgiving mean pretending nothing happened? (No; it means letting go of revenge and wishing them well.)", "What do we ask in ‘deliver us from evil’? (To be rescued from the evil one and all evil.)"],
    practice=["Role-play a situation: one student takes another’s pencil. The other says it hurt. The first apologizes. The second says, “I forgive you.” Practice with different scenarios.", "Talk about how it feels to be forgiven and to forgive.", "Draw a shield and write on it: “Deliver us from evil.” Around it, write the things you want protection from.", "Pray the last three petitions together."],
    close="Pray the whole Our Father with the doxology: “For the kingdom, the power and the glory are yours, now and forever.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Forgive someone this week.”")),
}
